{"id":"evt_144e44ae5001aoD70KsRIvvp6V","type":"message.part.updated","properties":{"sessionID":"ses_ebb1c1787ffe7Ldr1RIAkHyPYL","part":{"id":"prt_144e445bb001iyzsrXXy1Y5XjR","messageID":"msg_144e3f4d600150JJKJRecPzm79","sessionID":"ses_ebb1c1787ffe7Ldr1RIAkHyPYL","type":"text","text":"A git worktree is a feature that lets you check out multiple branches of the same repository into separate directories at the same time, each with its own working tree and index. This is different from the standard workflow where you switch branches in a single directory and lose your uncommitted changes or staging area in the process.\n\nUse a git worktree when you need to work on multiple branches simultaneously without constantly switching context—for example, comparing two implementations side-by-side, running tests on one branch while editing another, or keeping a clean copy of `main` available for reference while you develop a feature branch.","time":{"start":1792157173179,"end":1792157174501}},"time":1792157174501}}
